A = {
"eu165135": dict(
 bluf="Hobbes argued that political authority rests not on divine right but on an agreement among individuals, who give up their freedom to a sovereign in exchange for security. Written after civil war, Leviathan founded modern social contract theory and the secular justification of the state.",
 why="The English Civil War and decades of religious war across Europe convinced Hobbes that the worst political evil was the breakdown of order.",
 then="Locke and Rousseau answered with versions of the social contract that limited the sovereign or rooted power in the people, and Hobbes's materialism scandalized the clergy.",
 now="Arguments about trading liberty for security, from emergency powers to surveillance, still follow Hobbes's logic, and 'Hobbesian' describes a world of anarchy among states.",
 lens="Societies that have lived through chaos will accept strong authority; ask what kind of disorder a people fears most."),
"af165210": dict(
 bluf="The VOC's supply station at the Cape grew into a settler colony that took Khoikhoi and San land and relied on enslaved labor from Asia and Africa. It was the seed of white settlement in South Africa and of the racial order that ended in apartheid.",
 story="In 1652 Jan van Riebeeck landed at Table Bay to build a fort and gardens to supply Dutch ships sailing to Asia. From 1657 the Company released some employees as free farmers, who pushed into Khoikhoi grazing lands, and it imported enslaved people from Madagascar, Mozambique, India and the East Indies.",
 why="The Dutch East India Company needed a station to supply fresh food and water to its ships on the long voyage to Asia.",
 then="Wars and a smallpox epidemic (1713) destroyed Khoikhoi society. Dutch-speaking farmers spread inland and became the Afrikaners. Britain took the Cape in 1795 and for good in 1806, which led the Boers to trek inland in the 1830s.",
 now="South Africa's racial divisions, the Afrikaans language and the bitter politics of land ownership trace back to this colony.",
 lens="A supply station can become a settler colony, and once settlers need land, conflict with the original inhabitants follows."),
"eu165336": dict(
 bluf="Cromwell's Protectorate showed that a revolution against royal tyranny could end in rule by an army and its general. Its unpopularity made the return of the monarchy possible and left Britain with a lasting suspicion of standing armies.",
 why="Cromwell grew frustrated with Parliament's failure to agree on a new constitution or reforms, and the army was the real power in the state.",
 then="His rule brought toleration for many Protestant sects and quietly readmitted Jews to England (1656), but also military government and Puritan restrictions. His conquest of Ireland (1649-53) included massacres at Drogheda and Wexford and a massive transfer of land to Protestants. After his death in 1658 the regime collapsed.",
 now="He remains deeply controversial: a champion of Parliament to some, a war criminal in Irish memory.",
 lens="Revolutions often end with the strongest army in charge."),
"d-pyrenees": dict(
 bluf="The Treaty of the Pyrenees confirmed that France had replaced Spain as Europe's leading power and fixed much of the modern border between them. Louis XIV's marriage to a Spanish princess set up the succession crisis that triggered a European war forty years later.",
 why="Both kingdoms were exhausted, and France, with English help, had beaten Spain in 1658.",
 then="France gained Roussillon and Artois. The marriage gave the Bourbons a claim to the Spanish throne, which they pressed in 1700.",
 now="The Franco-Spanish border still largely follows the 1659 line, which split historic Catalonia: northern Catalonia (Roussillon) remains French.",
 lens="Dynastic marriages made as peace terms can become the cause of the next war."),
"eu166037": dict(
 bluf="The Restoration brought back the monarchy but not absolute rule: Charles II returned at Parliament's invitation and had to govern with it. The unresolved tension over Catholicism and royal power led to the Glorious Revolution of 1688.",
 why="Without Cromwell the republic fell into chaos, and many saw the monarchy as the only stable option.",
 then="Royalists took revenge on the regicides, the Church of England was re-established and Protestant dissenters were penalized. Charles II's Catholic sympathies and his brother James's open Catholicism revived fears of absolutism.",
 now="England's monarchy has survived ever since, with steadily less power.",
 lens="Restorations rarely restore the old order fully: returning rulers come back on new terms."),
"d-royalsociety": dict(
 bluf="The Royal Society made the experimental method an institution: claims had to be demonstrated, witnessed and published so others could check them. Its journal created the system of scientific publication modern science still uses.",
 why="Peace after the civil wars, and a circle of experimenters inspired by Francis Bacon's call to build knowledge from observation.",
 then="The Society's licence appeared on Newton's Principia in 1687, printed at Edmond Halley's expense.",
 now="Scientific journals and peer review descend from its Philosophical Transactions, and its motto, 'Take nobody's word for it', is still science's stance toward authority.",
 lens="Knowledge advances fastest where institutions exist to test and share claims in public."),
"as166120": dict(
 bluf="Kangxi consolidated Manchu rule over China, crushing the last internal rebellions, absorbing Taiwan and fixing the Russian border. His 61-year reign made the Qing a stable, expanding empire and won over much of the Chinese scholar elite.",
 why="His father, the Shunzhi Emperor, died of smallpox; Kangxi was chosen partly because he had already survived the disease.",
 then="He patronized Confucian scholarship, keeping Chinese elites invested in the dynasty, and tolerated Jesuits until a dispute with Rome over Chinese rites. Under his grandson Qianlong the empire reached its greatest extent.",
 now="He is remembered in China as a model ruler, the founder of the 'High Qing' era.",
 lens="Conquest dynasties last when they win over the conquered elite through its own institutions."),
"na16649": dict(
 bluf="England's seizure of New Amsterdam gave it an unbroken coastline from New England to the Chesapeake and a superb harbour. New York's Dutch commercial origins and diversity shaped what became America's financial capital.",
 why="England and the Dutch Republic were rivals for trade, and England wanted the Dutch colony that split its American colonies in two.",
 then="England now held an unbroken coast from Maine to the Carolinas; the Dutch briefly retook the city in 1673 but gave it up for good in 1674.",
 now="New York's trade-first, many-languages culture began under the Dutch.",
 lens="Trade rivalries become wars over the best ports."),
"eu166638": dict(
 bluf="The Great Fire destroyed most of medieval London and forced it to be rebuilt in brick and stone under new building rules. It sped the birth of fire insurance and modern urban regulation.",
 why="A fire at a bakery in Pudding Lane spread through tightly packed wooden houses after a dry summer, fanned by a strong east wind.",
 then="Christopher Wren rebuilt St Paul's and dozens of churches, and the Rebuilding Act of 1667 required new buildings to be of brick or stone.",
 now="It gave rise to the first fire insurance companies and to building codes of the kind cities still use.",
 lens="Disasters are often what finally forces cities to adopt safety rules."),
"sa-shivaji": dict(
 bluf="Shivaji built a Maratha kingdom in western India that challenged the Mughal Empire at its height. The Maratha wars drained Aurangzeb's resources and hastened Mughal decline, and the Marathas became India's strongest power in the 18th century.",
 why="Resistance to Mughal and Deccan sultanate rule, and the ambitions of Maratha warrior families.",
 then="Aurangzeb spent his last 25 years fighting the Marathas in the Deccan. After his death the Marathas expanded across much of India, until defeated by the Afghans at Panipat (1761) and finally by the British (1818).",
 now="Shivaji is a hero of Marathi and Hindu nationalist politics, and Mumbai's main airport and railway station bear his name.",
 lens="Guerrilla war from mountain strongholds can bleed a great empire for decades."),
"na167510": dict(
 bluf="King Philip's War was, relative to population, one of the deadliest wars in American history, and it ended Native independence in southern New England. Its pattern of land pressure, war, enslavement and removal repeated across the continent.",
 why="Colonists kept taking land and imposing their law on Native peoples, while Native leaders saw their autonomy shrinking with each treaty.",
 trig="Plymouth's execution in June 1675 of three Wampanoag men for killing John Sassamon, a Christian Native informer.",
 then="Dozens of towns burned on both sides. Metacom was killed, and many Native captives were sold into slavery in the Caribbean. The Native nations of southern New England never recovered their independence.",
 now="It set a pattern of frontier war and removal that repeated across the continent.",
 lens="Growing settler populations turn coexistence into conflict over land."),
"na167611": dict(
 bluf="When poor white and Black Virginians joined in revolt against the colonial elite, planters responded by turning to African slavery and writing racial divisions into law. Many historians see it as a turning point in the making of America's racial order.",
 why="Frontier settlers wanted land and war on Native peoples, and resented a governor and elite who controlled land and the Native trade, at a time of low tobacco prices.",
 then="Frightened planters shifted from indentured servants to enslaved Africans and passed laws giving poor whites privileges over Black people, dividing workers by race.",
 now="Historians point to this moment as a key origin of America's racial hierarchy.",
 lens="Elites facing a revolt that crosses group lines often split the rebels along racial or ethnic lines."),
"na168212": dict(
 bluf="Pennsylvania was founded on religious tolerance and drew a diverse mix of settlers, becoming a model of pluralism that shaped American ideals. Philadelphia, its planned capital, became the political centre of the American Revolution.",
 why="Charles II repaid a debt to Penn's father with land, and Penn wanted a refuge for Quakers and a 'holy experiment' in tolerance.",
 then="Pennsylvania drew Germans, Scots-Irish and many others and became the most diverse colony. Penn's fair dealing with the Lenape was later undermined by his sons, notably in the fraudulent Walking Purchase of 1737.",
 now="Religious freedom and pluralism became core American ideals, and Philadelphia hosted both the Declaration of Independence and the Constitutional Convention.",
 lens="Tolerance can be an economic strategy: open colonies attract settlers and capital."),
"as168321": dict(
 bluf="The Qing conquest of Taiwan in 1683 brought the island under Chinese imperial rule for the first time. That two-century incorporation is the historical basis of Beijing's claim to Taiwan today.",
 why="Koxinga's family used Taiwan as a base for Ming loyalist resistance and maritime trade, and the Qing needed to eliminate it to secure the coast.",
 then="The Qing at first ruled Taiwan reluctantly as part of Fujian province and restricted migration, but Han Chinese settlers came anyway and displaced Indigenous peoples. Taiwan became a province in 1885 and was ceded to Japan in 1895.",
 now="Whether Taiwan is part of China is one of the most dangerous flashpoints in world politics, and both sides draw on this history.",
 lens="Territorial claims rest on history, but which history counts is itself contested: 1683, 1895 and 1949 tell different stories."),
"eu168339": dict(
 bluf="The relief of Vienna in 1683 was the turning point between Ottoman expansion and Ottoman retreat in Europe. Within 16 years the Habsburgs had taken Hungary, and the question of what would replace Ottoman power in the Balkans began to shape European politics.",
 story="In July 1683 Grand Vizier Kara Mustafa besieged Vienna with a huge army. After two months, a relief army of Habsburg, German and Polish troops under the Polish king Jan Sobieski broke the siege on September 12 with one of the largest cavalry charges in history.",
 why="Kara Mustafa sought the prestige of taking the Habsburg capital, encouraged by a Hungarian revolt against the Habsburgs. France, keen to weaken the Habsburgs, stayed out.",
 then="A Holy League of Austria, Poland, Venice and later Russia drove the Ottomans back, and at Karlowitz (1699) the Ottomans gave up Hungary. Kara Mustafa was executed on the sultan's orders.",
 now="1683 is still invoked in European arguments over Islam and migration, and the Ottoman legacy in the Balkans remains politically charged.",
 lens="Imperial decline shows first at the frontier: watch where expansion stops."),
"eu168740": dict(
 bluf="Newton's Principia showed that the same mathematical laws govern motion on Earth and in the heavens. It completed the Scientific Revolution, made the universe appear predictable, and gave the Enlightenment its model of reason uncovering natural laws.",
 story="Prompted by Edmond Halley, who paid for its printing, Isaac Newton published the Principia in 1687. It set out three laws of motion and the law of universal gravitation, and used them to explain Kepler's planetary orbits, the tides and the paths of comets.",
 why="Kepler and Galileo had described how planets and falling bodies move but not why. Newton set out to derive the orbits from one general law of force.",
 then="Newtonian physics dominated science for two centuries and inspired thinkers to look for similar 'laws' of society, economics and politics, a foundation of Enlightenment thought.",
 now="Engineers still use Newton's laws to build bridges and launch rockets; only at very high speeds or very small scales do Einstein's relativity and quantum mechanics take over.",
 lens="The most powerful theories unify: when one explanation covers things once thought separate, a revolution follows."),
"eu168841": dict(
 bluf="The Glorious Revolution replaced a Catholic king with Protestant co-monarchs on terms set by Parliament. It established parliamentary supremacy, constitutional monarchy and a state able to borrow cheaply, the foundations of Britain's 18th-century power and a model for American constitutionalism.",
 why="James II was promoting Catholics, setting aside laws by royal prerogative and building a standing army. William of Orange wanted England's resources for his war against Louis XIV.",
 trig="The birth of James's son in June 1688, which promised a Catholic dynasty, and the trial of seven bishops who refused to read his declaration of toleration.",
 then="The Bill of Rights (1689) limited royal power; Parliament now controlled taxes and the army and met every year. Britain joined the wars against France, financed by the Bank of England (1694) and a permanent national debt. In Ireland and Scotland the change was contested by force.",
 now="Britain's constitutional monarchy, and the American founders' ideas of limited government, rest on this settlement; economists cite it as a case of political institutions enabling growth.",
 lens="Revolutions succeed when a foreign power's strategic interest lines up with domestic grievances."),
"eu168942b": dict(
 bluf="Locke argued that government exists by consent to protect natural rights to life, liberty and property, and that people may resist rulers who violate them. His ideas became the theoretical core of liberal democracy and of the American Revolution.",
 why="Locke wrote to justify resistance to James II and the constitutional settlement that followed.",
 then="His ideas spread across Europe and the American colonies.",
 now="The Declaration of Independence echoes Locke's 'life, liberty and property', and debates over property rights, consent and legitimate rebellion still use his terms.",
 lens="Legitimacy comes from protecting people's rights: ask what a government offers in return for obedience."),
"d-nerchinsk": dict(
 bluf="Nerchinsk was China's first treaty with a European state, and it was made on equal terms. It fixed the Russia-China border for 170 years, keeping Russia out of the Amur valley and securing the Qing's Manchurian homeland.",
 why="The Qing wanted to secure Manchuria and keep Russia away from the Mongols; Russia wanted trade.",
 then="The border held until Russia, taking advantage of Qing weakness, annexed the Amur region and the Pacific coast in 1858-60.",
 now="The contrast between Nerchinsk and the later 'unequal treaties' remains part of Chinese historical memory.",
 lens="Treaties reflect the balance of power when signed; when the balance shifts, borders follow."),
"eu168942": dict(
 bluf="The Bill of Rights turned the outcome of the Glorious Revolution into law: the crown could not tax, suspend laws or keep a peacetime army without Parliament's consent. It made Parliament the centre of British government.",
 why="To settle the Glorious Revolution and prevent another James II.",
 then="Parliament, not the crown, became the centre of British government.",
 now="The US Bill of Rights copied its ban on 'cruel and unusual punishments' almost word for word.",
 lens="Writing limits on power into law makes them harder to reverse than promises."),
"d-boyne": dict(
 bluf="The Boyne secured William III's hold on Ireland and the Protestant succession in Britain. For Ireland it began a century of Protestant Ascendancy that shut Catholics out of land and power, a division at the root of the Northern Ireland conflict.",
 why="James II, deposed in 1688, used Catholic Ireland as a base to win back his thrones, with French support.",
 then="Penal laws shut Irish Catholics out of power and land ownership for more than a century.",
 now="Orange Order parades in Northern Ireland still commemorate the battle every July 12.",
 lens="When a community still marches to celebrate a 300-year-old victory, the conflict behind it is still alive."),
"d-calcutta": dict(
 bluf="Calcutta gave the East India Company a fortified base in Bengal, the richest province of Mughal India. From there the Company won Bengal in 1757 and began its conquest of India, and Calcutta became the capital of British India.",
 why="Bengal produced silk, cotton cloth and saltpetre for gunpowder, and the Company needed a secure port there.",
 then="Calcutta became the base from which Clive won Bengal in 1757, and the capital of British India until 1911.",
 now="Kolkata is one of India's largest cities; whether Charnock should be called its founder is still debated.",
 lens="Ports chosen for trade become capitals of empire when the traders turn to conquest."),
"na169213": dict(
 bluf="The Salem trials showed how fear, local feuds and courts that accepted unverifiable evidence could produce mass injustice. Their collapse discredited 'spectral evidence' and pushed colonial courts toward stricter standards of proof.",
 why="Fear, local feuds, the trauma of frontier war with Native peoples and France, and religious anxiety.",
 then="Massachusetts later apologized and paid compensation to the victims' families.",
 now="'Witch hunt' became shorthand for mass hysteria, from the McCarthy era to today.",
 lens="Panics feed on accusations that cannot be disproved: watch for charges that need no evidence."),
"la169310": dict(
 bluf="Brazil's gold rush shifted the colony's population and economy inland and sharply increased demand for enslaved Africans. Much of the gold flowed through Portugal to Britain, helping finance Britain's commercial expansion.",
 why="Bandeirantes searching the interior for captives and precious metals struck gold in Minas Gerais.",
 then="Minas Gerais became Brazil's richest region, and the capital moved from Salvador to Rio de Janeiro (1763), the port for the mines. Under the Methuen Treaty (1703) Portugal bought British cloth and paid the difference in Brazilian gold.",
 now="Minas Gerais is still a mining state; the dam disasters at Mariana (2015) and Brumadinho (2019) show the industry's continuing costs.",
 lens="Resource booms move people and capital quickly; follow where the profits end up, which may be far away."),
"eu169443": dict(
 bluf="The Bank of England let the British state borrow cheaply and reliably by turning war debt into a trusted asset backed by Parliament. That fiscal strength let Britain outspend and outfight richer France for a century.",
 why="William III needed money for the war against Louis XIV, and investors wanted a secure return backed by Parliament's taxes.",
 then="A permanent national debt, serviced by taxes Parliament approved, became the backbone of Britain's war-making state. The Bank later became the lender of last resort for the banking system.",
 now="Almost every country now has a central bank built on this model.",
 lens="In long wars, credit can matter more than wealth: the side that can borrow cheaply can fight longer."),
"d-zenta": dict(
 bluf="Zenta was the decisive Habsburg victory of the Great Turkish War and forced the Ottomans to accept heavy territorial losses at Karlowitz. It established Austria as the dominant power in the Danube basin.",
 why="The Habsburgs had been driving the Ottomans back since the relief of Vienna in 1683.",
 then="The defeat forced the Ottomans to make peace at Karlowitz in 1699.",
 now="Prince Eugene became one of Europe's most celebrated generals, and Austria's identity as a Danubian great power was forged in these wars.",
 lens="Armies are most vulnerable while moving, above all when crossing rivers."),
"af169811": dict(
 bluf="Oman's capture of Fort Jesus ended Portuguese power on the Swahili coast north of Mozambique and began Omani dominance in East Africa. It tied the coast more closely to Arabia and set up Zanzibar's later rise as a centre of the clove and slave trades.",
 why="Oman, having expelled the Portuguese from Muscat in 1650, was building its own Indian Ocean empire.",
 then="Oman controlled the coast and its trade for 150 years, and in 1840 its sultan moved his capital to Zanzibar.",
 now="Omani influence shaped Swahili culture and architecture, and Fort Jesus is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.",
 lens="Regional powers often replace a declining European empire before the next European empire arrives."),
"d-karlowitz": dict(
 bluf="Karlowitz was the first major treaty in which the Ottomans permanently gave up large territories to Christian powers. It marked the Ottoman shift from expansion to defence, and Austria's rise as a great power on the Danube.",
 why="Sixteen years of defeats after the failure at Vienna.",
 then="The Ottoman Empire turned to defence, and Austria became the leading power on the Danube. English and Dutch mediation also set a precedent for European involvement in Ottoman affairs.",
 now="Historians see Karlowitz as the start of the long Ottoman retreat from Europe.",
 lens="The first treaty in which an empire permanently gives up land often marks the start of its long retreat."),
"af170112": dict(
 bluf="Osei Tutu united Akan states into the Asante confederacy around the Golden Stool, which gave the new state a sacred, shared identity. Asante became a major West African power whose gold and war captives tied it to the Atlantic economy, and later Britain's toughest opponent on the Gold Coast.",
 why="The Akan states united under Osei Tutu to throw off the rule of their powerful neighbour, Denkyira.",
 then="Asante defeated Denkyira in 1701 and expanded, selling gold and captives to Europeans on the coast for guns and goods. After Britain abolished the slave trade, the two fought a series of wars, and Britain annexed Asante in 1901 after the War of the Golden Stool.",
 now="The Asantehene, the Asante king, still holds great cultural authority in Ghana.",
 lens="Shared sacred symbols can bind separate communities into a state more durably than conquest."),
"eu170144": dict(
 bluf="The war prevented the union of the French and Spanish crowns and made the balance of power the organizing principle of European politics. Britain came out as the strongest naval and commercial power, with Gibraltar and access to the Spanish American slave trade.",
 why="Fear that a France and Spain ruled by one Bourbon family would dominate Europe and the Americas.",
 trig="Charles II of Spain died childless in 1700 and left his throne to Louis XIV's grandson, Philip.",
 then="Philip kept Spain but renounced the French throne. Britain gained Gibraltar, Minorca and the asiento, the contract to supply enslaved Africans to Spanish America.",
 now="Spain's Bourbon dynasty, which still reigns, dates from this war, and Catalonia's defeat in 1714 is commemorated on its national day, September 11.",
 lens="Coalitions form against any power that threatens to dominate the whole system."),
"d-stpetersburg": dict(
 bluf="St. Petersburg was Peter the Great's declaration that Russia would be a European, maritime power. The new capital on the Baltic turned the Russian state westward for two centuries.",
 why="Peter wanted a Baltic port and a European-style capital away from old Moscow and its traditions.",
 then="Tens of thousands of conscripted laborers built it, many dying of disease. It became the capital in 1712 and remained so until 1918.",
 now="Its historic centre is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, and the city embodies Russia's long argument over whether it belongs to Europe.",
 lens="Moving a capital is a statement of strategy: watch where a state puts its centre of power."),
"as170722": dict(
 bluf="Aurangzeb's death began the Mughal Empire's rapid fragmentation. Overstretched by decades of war in the Deccan and having alienated many Hindu nobles with his religious policies, the empire broke into regional states that the British later exploited.",
 why="He died at 88 after decades of costly campaigns and religious policies that cost him the loyalty of many nobles.",
 then="His sons fought over the throne. Regional powers, from the Marathas to the nawabs of Bengal and Hyderabad, became independent in practice, and the East India Company exploited the divisions.",
 now="Aurangzeb is one of the most fiercely debated figures in Indian politics today.",
 lens="Empires at their territorial peak are often already overstretched: watch the cost of the last conquests."),
"eu170745": dict(
 bluf="The Acts of Union merged England and Scotland into Great Britain, with one state, one parliament and one free-trade zone. Scotland gained access to England's empire; England secured its northern border. The union became the base of British imperial power.",
 why="Scotland was nearly bankrupt after its failed colony at Darien in Panama, and England wanted to stop Scotland choosing a different (possibly Catholic Stuart) monarch and allying with France.",
 then="Scots played an outsized role in the British Empire, as soldiers, administrators and merchants, and in the Enlightenment.",
 now="Scotland voted to stay in the UK in 2014, but independence remains a live question, sharpened by Brexit.",
 lens="Unions are often driven by economic crisis on one side and security fears on the other, and they last only while both sides still benefit."),
"eu170946": dict(
 bluf="Poltava destroyed Sweden's army and its standing as a great power, and made Russia the dominant power in northeastern Europe. It also crushed the hopes of the Cossack leader Ivan Mazepa, who had sided with Sweden against Moscow.",
 why="The Great Northern War over control of the Baltic.",
 then="Russia won the Baltic coast at the Treaty of Nystad (1721), and Peter proclaimed himself emperor. Mazepa's defection led Moscow to curtail Cossack autonomy in Ukraine.",
 now="Poltava is remembered as a Russian triumph, a Swedish disaster and, in Ukraine, a lost chance for independence; Mazepa remains a contested figure.",
 lens="Small powers that bet on an outside protector against a large neighbour risk everything if the protector loses."),
"eu171347": dict(
 bluf="The Utrecht treaties ended the War of the Spanish Succession by keeping France and Spain under separate crowns. They made the balance of power an explicit principle of European diplomacy and launched Britain as the leading maritime and commercial power.",
 why="After 12 years of war, every power was exhausted, and Britain's new Tory government wanted peace.",
 then="Austria gained the Spanish Netherlands and Spanish Italy, while Savoy and Prussia rose in status. Britain gained Gibraltar, Minorca, Nova Scotia, Newfoundland and the asiento. France was contained but remained powerful.",
 now="Gibraltar, Britain's prize, still causes friction with Spain.",
 lens="Peace settlements that balance rival powers last longer than ones that crush the loser."),
"eu172048": dict(
 bluf="The South Sea Bubble was Britain's first great financial crash, a speculative mania built on a scheme to swap government debt for company shares. It showed how financial innovation, insider dealing and political corruption can inflate a bubble, and how the state then has to clean up.",
 why="Excitement over company shares, fed by insiders and bribed politicians, created a speculative mania.",
 then="Parliament passed the Bubble Act restricting new companies, and Robert Walpole rose to power by managing the fallout.",
 now="Every bubble since, from railway mania to the dot-com boom to crypto, gets compared to it.",
 lens="When politicians, insiders and the public all profit from rising prices, no one has an incentive to stop a bubble."),
"jp-rangaku": dict(
 bluf="Allowing in Western books, except Christian ones, let Japanese scholars study European science through Dutch texts for over a century. This 'Dutch learning' gave Japan a base of scientific knowledge and translators that made rapid modernization after 1853 possible.",
 lens="Even closed societies can import knowledge selectively; the channels left open matter."),
"eu172149": dict(
 bluf="Peter the Great forcibly modernized Russia's state, army and elite along Western European lines and declared it an empire. He made Russia a European great power, but his coercive, top-down model of modernization set a pattern later rulers repeated.",
 story="Peter I toured Western Europe in 1697-98, partly incognito, studying shipbuilding and government. At home he forced nobles to shave their beards and dress in European style, built a navy, reorganized the army and administration, put the Church under state control and founded St. Petersburg. In 1721, after defeating Sweden, he took the title Emperor of All Russia.",
 why="Russia lagged militarily and technologically behind Western Europe and Sweden, and Peter believed survival and greatness required copying Western methods.",
 trig="Victory over Sweden in the Great Northern War, sealed by the Treaty of Nystad in 1721.",
 then="Russia became a European great power. Service to the state became the basis of noble rank (the Table of Ranks, 1722), and serfdom deepened to pay for it all. The elite became Westernized while the peasants did not, a cultural divide that lasted until 1917.",
 now="Russia still argues over whether it is European, Eurasian or a civilization of its own, and Putin has compared himself to Peter as a ruler 'returning' Russian lands.",
 lens="Top-down modernization can work fast, but it widens the gap between a Westernized elite and the rest of society."),
"as173923": dict(
 bluf="Nader Shah's sack of Delhi exposed the Mughal Empire's military weakness and emptied its treasury. It sped the empire's collapse into regional powers and showed European companies how vulnerable India had become.",
 why="Nader Shah was drawn by Mughal wealth and a weak emperor, and Mughal border defences had collapsed.",
 then="Mughal weakness was laid bare; regional rulers and the British and French companies drew their own conclusions.",
 now="The Koh-i-Noor diamond, taken in the sack, is now in the British Crown Jewels, and India still asks for it back.",
 lens="One devastating raid can reveal a power's weakness to everyone watching, and invite more predators."),
"na173914": dict(
 bluf="The Stono Rebellion was the largest slave revolt in Britain's mainland colonies. The harsh laws that followed show how slave societies answered resistance by tightening control.",
 why="Brutal conditions, and Spanish Florida's offer of freedom to runaways, a deliberate tactic against Britain.",
 then="South Carolina's Negro Act of 1740 barred enslaved people from gathering, growing their own food, earning money or learning to read.",
 now="Stono shows that enslaved Africans resisted from the start.",
 lens="Rival powers exploit each other's internal weaknesses: Spain offered freedom to Britain's enslaved people to destabilize its colonies."),
}
